    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes green. Let me summarize the change.\n\n## Summary\n\nI converted owner deletion to a soft-delete. `DELETE /api/owners/{id}` now flags the record instead of removing it, and the create endpoint's duplicate/identity checks ignore flagged owners. **`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass** (including the two new acceptance tests: `coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains` and `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck`).\n\n### Changes, each in its natural home\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `deleted` persistent field (`@Column(name=\"deleted\", nullable=false)`, a primitive `boolean` so a new owner defaults to `false`) with `isDeleted()` / `setDeleted(...)`.\n\n2. **`rest/function/owner/DeleteOwner.java`** \u2014 the delete step now sets `deleted = true` and `save`s, instead of `ownerRepository.delete(owner)`. Loading (`LoadOwner`) is untouched, so `GET` still returns the retained record with `deleted: true`.\n\n3. **`rest/function/owner/Households.java`** \u2014 `membersOf(...)`, the shared household query behind the duplicate block (`EnsureUniqueHousehold`), the possible-duplicate flag (`AssignOwnerPossibleDuplicate`) and household size, now filters out soft-deleted owners. Filtering at this one shared helper covers all three household checks without duplication.\n\n4. **`rest/function/owner/AssignOwnerNamesakeCount.java`** \u2014 the namesake (identity) count now also excludes soft-deleted owners.\n\n5. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name (create response \u2192 `false`, post-delete GET \u2192 `true`).\n\n6. **`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 added `deleted BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE` to the `owners` table. The tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema file is what actually creates the column; the `DEFAULT FALSE` also keeps existing seed rows live.\n\nOne deliberate non-change: the `jdbc`-profile repository's partial `save` (which never wrote the newer owner columns) was left as-is since it's already incomplete for this app's feature set and the active profile is `spring-data-jpa`.",
